        "aiSupplement": {
          "explanation": [
            {
              "text": "Two melanic parents can produce typical offspring if both carry a recessive typical allele. The heterozygous cross gives three dominant-phenotype combinations for every recessive-phenotype combination.",
              "refs": [
                "ms:a#1",
                "ms:a#2",
                "ms:a#3",
                "syllabus:16.2(2)"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reasoningChain": [
            "Infer the parental genotypes from the recessive offspring.",
            "Combine gametes and connect offspring genotypes to phenotypes."
          ],
          "inferredMistakes": [
            {
              "text": "Giving both melanic parents homozygous dominant genotypes.",
              "basis": "inferred",
              "refs": [
                "ms:a#1",
                "ms:a#2",
                "ms:a#3",
                "syllabus:16.2(2)"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "type": "extended"
      },
      {
        "key": "b",
        "label": "(b)",
        "source": {
          "qp": 22,
          "ms": 15
        },
        "stem": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "In a similar experiment, two melanic North American moths were crossed together. The colours of the offspring were 10 typical and 31 melanic.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 22
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What can be concluded about the allele that causes the melanic form in the moth populations in <b>both</b> continents?",
            "source": {
              "qp": 22
            }
          }
        ],
        "marks": 1,
        "markScheme": {
          "marks": 1,
          "instruction": null,
          "guidance": [],
          "points": [
            {
              "n": 1,
              "text": "it / (melanic) allele, is dominant <b>; </b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            }
          ]
        },
        "er": [],
        "topics": [
          {
            "ref": "16.2(1)",
            "role": "primary"
          }
        ],
        "aiSupplement": {
          "explanation": [
            {
              "text": "The approximate 3:1 offspring phenotype ratio is consistent with a dominant melanic allele carried by heterozygous parents. A similar phenotype ratio does not by itself establish that the two continental alleles occupy the same locus.",
              "refs": [
                "ms:b#1",
                "syllabus:16.2(1)"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reasoningChain": [
            "Recognise the dominant-phenotype inheritance pattern.",
            "Limit the conclusion to dominance rather than locus identity."
          ],
          "inferredMistakes": [
            {
              "text": "Concluding that similar ratios prove that both continental alleles are at the same locus.",
              "basis": "inferred",
              "refs": [
                "ms:b#1",
                "syllabus:16.2(1)"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "type": "short"
      },
      {
        "key": "c",
        "label": "(c)",
        "source": {
          "qp": 23
        },
        "stem": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Researchers did not know if the allele causing the melanic form in European moths occurred at the same locus as the allele causing the melanic form in North American moths. To find out, they carried out the following crosses:",
            "source": {
              "qp": 23
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "list",
            "items": [
              "Cross 1: European moths that were heterozygous at the European melanic locus only were crossed with North American moths that were heterozygous at the North American melanic locus only.",
              "Cross 2: The melanic and the typical offspring of cross 1 were mated together."
            ]
          }
        ],
        "parts": [
          {
            "key": "c.i",
            "label": "(i)",
            "source": {
              "qp": 23,
              "ms": 15
            },
            "stem": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Explain why cross 2 is a test cross.",
                "source": {
                  "qp": 23
                }
              }
            ],
            "marks": 1,
            "markScheme": {
              "marks": 1,
              "instruction": null,
              "guidance": [],
              "points": [
                {
                  "n": 1,
                  "text": "melanic / dominant phenotype, is crossed with homozygous recessive <b>; </b>",
                  "numberedInMs": false
                }
              ]
            },
            "er": [],
            "topics": [
              {
                "ref": "16.2(4)",
                "role": "primary"
              }
            ],
            "aiSupplement": {
              "explanation": [
                {
                  "text": "A test cross mates an individual showing the dominant phenotype with a homozygous recessive individual. The recessive parent makes the other parent’s allele contribution detectable in offspring phenotypes.",
                  "refs": [
                    "ms:c.i#1",
                    "syllabus:16.2(4)"
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "reasoningChain": [
                "Identify the dominant-phenotype and recessive-phenotype parents.",
                "State the homozygous recessive genotype required for a test cross."
              ],
              "inferredMistakes": [
                {
                  "text": "Defining any cross between different phenotypes as a test cross.",
                  "basis": "inferred",
                  "refs": [
                    "ms:c.i#1",
                    "syllabus:16.2(4)"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            "type": "short"
          },
